I describe impostor syndrome as having  three elements to it the first is the  belief that other people have an  inflated view of your own abilities or  skills are far more inflated view than  you have in yourself the second factor  is that you have this intense fear that  you'll be found out and exposed as a  fake and the third element is that you  constantly attribute your success you do  recognize to other factors outside of  your own abilities or talent when plas  syndrome can feel like a dissonance or a  disconnect but if you have it a lot and  you're constantly feeling that way then  the disconnect between what you see as  yourself and your public view just grows  bigger and you feel fake and you're  feeling impostor my imposter syndrome is  something that affects so many of us  that actually to call it a syndrome is  probably a little bit inaccurate because  that suggests it's kind of mental health  condition or there's something wrong  with us but you know when it's 70  percent of us it's probably more likely  to be something that is almost normal  really you can't have impostor syndrome  without being successful you've got  people like Tom Hanks who you'd think  well they've made it they must be  confident in their abilities no matter  who we are no matter what we've done  there comes a point where you think how  did I get here and am I going to be able  to continue this when are they going to  discover that I am in fact a fraud and  take everything away from me  I think a little bit of self-doubt is  very good for self development the  imposter syndrome people are the ones  double-checking their work working  really hard trying to get everything  right never satisfied always looking for  more training opportunities for more  learning opportunities and those are all  potentially good things there are a  number of factors from childhood that  may contribute to impostor syndrome when  children are put on a pedestal that's a  very very difficult premise to look to  live up to and that can help make you  feel an imposter because if you're not  perfect then you're not the golden child  that they think you are  the term imposter syndrome was first  coined in 90  in 78 by Pauline Clanton Susanne  Eames in a paper that they entitled  imposter phenomenon in high achieving  women so it was originally something  that was thought to only affect women  when we talk about imposter syndrome in  the workplace I see that as the same for  men and women but I do see a lot of men  who don't think their mannina social  media has a huge role to play in  impostor syndrome we can see everybody  else's successful lives the more we  present our best self to the world the  more other people will develop impostor  syndrome because they're never going to  measure up I've had people who wanted to  give up their jobs their high-flying  jobs because they fear so much being  found out that they'd rather give it up  and handing their notice even though  objectively that they're not fake and  objectively they are successful if you  think that you are suffering from  impostor syndrome actually look  objectively at your successes write down  the things that you are successful at  and then look at all the reasons why you  might have achieved that success assign  a percentage scores to how much you  think that factor contributed to that  achievement you can mistakenly attribute  all of it to work but when you put it  down on paper you'll realize how  ridiculous that is so it's a bit of a  reality check and it makes you stop and  acknowledge your successes Impostors  feel that they can't accept anything  that's less than perfect learn to make  mistakes and to accept failure you don't  have to be brilliant all the time that  you can be good enough  thanks for watching don't forget to  subscribe and click the bail to receive  notifications for new videos see you  again soon
 

